{
  "statement": "Treating the absence of documented runtime monitoring frameworks as a legal design defect misapplies industrial product liability to adaptive intelligence. Under the Restatement (Third) of Torts Section 2(b), a plaintiff proves a design defect by showing a reasonable alternative design would have prevented foreseeable harm. But emergent behavior in modern models is not a manufacturing flaw you can stamp out on a static assembly line. Software teams protect users through live telemetry, canary deployments, and staged rollouts, not through compliance paperwork filed before deployment. Mandating exhaustive pre-deployment monitoring documents turns the legal system into a bottleneck that penalizes fast, adaptive engineering while failing to catch novel failure modes in production.\n\nThis standard creates a regulatory moat that benefits dominant tech incumbents while crushing decentralized innovation. Large corporate labs easily absorb hundred-page compliance mandates and static audit trails. In contrast, academic labs, open-source developers, and seed-stage startups lack the compliance budgets to generate exhaustive operational filings for every model update. When Congress enacted Section 230 in 1996, permissionless deployment unlocked trillion-dollar digital industries. Applying strict design defect liability to missing monitoring documentation will do the exact opposite, concentrating algorithmic capability into three or four platform monopolies with the legal budgets to survive the paperwork.\n\nProponents of strict monitoring liability offer a serious argument: unmonitored deployments in high-stakes environments like hospital triage or algorithmic trading can trigger catastrophic, irreversible failures before operators notice the drift. That risk is real, but static compliance documentation does not fix it. Regulators should enforce outcome-based safety standards through market mechanisms and targeted liability insurance rather than procedural documentation mandates. In 1993, the FAA adopted performance-based navigation standards rather than dictating specific cockpit hardware. The Federal Trade Commission and state attorneys general can already penalize concrete, observable harms under Section 5 authority without freezing software delivery pipelines.\n\nEvery month regulators delay autonomous systems behind speculative paperwork requirements carries a measurable human toll. Autonomous vehicles reduced fatal crash rates by roughly 85 percent compared to human drivers in 2023 commercial fleet data across millions of miles. Freezing diagnostic AI deployments behind precautionary certification frameworks leaves thousands of preventable patient misdiagnoses in place every year. If you choose to classify missing operational paperwork as an actionable design defect, you are trading tangible, immediate lives saved for the illusion of procedural safety.",
