}","evaluate":"{"topic_resolution":{"restated_question":"Courts must determine whether failing to provide operational monitoring tools for artificial intelligence systems creates product liability when unpredictable model behaviors cause harm.","where_it_landed":"The debate establishes that omitting runtime monitoring creates legal exposure whenever upstream vendors block downstream operators from inspecting safety-critical failures. Safety advocates argue that any commercial model sold without standard telemetry interfaces suffers from a design defect under tort law. Accelerationists maintain that general-purpose foundation models operate as raw computational inputs rather than finished consumer products, which exempts them from traditional product liability. Skeptics offer a middle ground: liability attaches only when vendors sell proprietary models into high-risk pipelines while contractually barring users from building their own diagnostic tools.","what_would_resolve_it":"Empirical measurements showing whether open diagnostic interfaces introduce proprietary intellectual property leaks or measurable latency penalties across real-world clinical and industrial systems."},"preferences":[{"conflict":"Whether omitting standardized diagnostic telemetry interfaces from commercial foundation models constitutes an actionable legal design defect under Restatement (Third) of Torts § 2(b).","claim_ids":["C1","C2","C3"],"prevails":"C3","criterion":"logical_validity","rationale":"The skeptic position correctly distinguishes between general-purpose model distribution and restrictive commercial licensing that actively prevents downstream risk mitigation. Restatement (Third) of Torts § 2(b) requires a plaintiff to demonstrate a reasonable alternative design, which safety advocates establish by pointing to lightweight mathematical drift telemetry. However, treating every foundation model as an inherently defective product overreaches because downstream developers adapt general-purpose weights for non-critical uses. Holding vendors liable when they contractually lock downstream operators out of diagnostic logs targets the exact mechanism that causes unmitigated runtime failure.","what_would_change_this":"Appellate court rulings establishing that raw foundation model weights constitute finished commercial products rather than component inputs under strict liability doctrine."},{"conflict":"Whether federal policymakers should empower horizontal regulators like the Federal Trade Commission to mandate diagnostic telemetry standards or rely exclusively on domain-specific sectoral agencies.","claim_ids":["C5","C6"],"prevails":"C6","criterion":"implementation_specificity","rationale":"Sector-specific watchdogs possess the technical context necessary to define actionable risk thresholds for their operational environments. The Food and Drug Administration already enforces clinical software validation rules, whereas the Federal Trade Commission lacks engineering staff to evaluate machine learning telemetry in specialized medical devices. Blanket horizontal mandates risk creating rigid compliance procedures that entrench the largest cloud providers while failing to address specialized failure modes. Sectoral regulators can calibrate monitoring requirements to the exact hazard profile of each deployment environment.","what_would_change_this":"Evidence that foundation model developers systematically exploit jurisdictional gaps between sectoral agencies to deploy unmonitored autonomous agents."},{"conflict":"Whether pre-market diagnostic mandates prevent more real-world harm than the human cost of delayed deployment in medical and industrial applications.","claim_ids":["C7","C8"],"prevails":"C8","criterion":"empirical_evidence","rationale":"Proponents of diagnostic telemetry demonstrate that mathematical monitoring tracks out-of-distribution drift without harvesting private data or halting continuous software iteration. Accelerationists assert that compliance reviews increase patient mortality, but they provide no operational data showing that basic telemetry logging slows algorithm execution. Telemetry interfaces operate at inference time alongside models without requiring lengthy administrative pre-clearance. Verifiable runtime telemetry gives clinicians immediate warning when a diagnostic model encounters patient data outside its training distribution.","what_would_change_this":"Clinical trial data demonstrating that pre-market compliance documentation for telemetry logging delays life-saving diagnostic software deployments by measurable multi-year periods."}],"policy_implications":[{"disagreement":"Whether federal agencies should mandate standard diagnostic telemetry interfaces for all commercial foundation models.","policy_refs":["pol-028","pol-035","pol-093"],"positions":[{"pover":"safetyist","stance":"Supports mandatory transparency rules and telemetry standards under pol-035 and directs federal research funding under pol-028 and pol-093 toward standardized runtime interpretability tools."},{"pover":"accelerationist","stance":"Opposes mandatory telemetry standards as burdensome compliance moats, favoring targeted research grants under pol-028 and pol-093 over rigid operational mandates."},{"pover":"skeptic","stance":"Supports targeted transparency rules under pol-035 only for models sold into safety-critical sectors, while funding open diagnostic frameworks through pol-093."}],"implication":"Congress should direct the National Institute of Standards and Technology to publish voluntary telemetry interface guidelines under pol-093, while sectoral regulators mandate these interfaces only in high-risk applications under pol-035."},{"disagreement":"Whether regulatory agencies should issue temporary compliance waivers to prevent diagnostic telemetry rules from stalling high-stakes artificial intelligence deployments.","policy_refs":["pol-1002","pol-028"],"positions":[{"pover":"accelerationist","stance":"Supports granting broad regulatory waivers under pol-1002 to fast-track clinical and industrial artificial intelligence tools."},{"pover":"safetyist","stance":"Opposes waivers under pol-1002 because unmonitored systems deploy unquantified failure risks onto patients and consumers."},{"pover":"skeptic","stance":"Supports conditional waivers under pol-1002 only when operators implement third-party runtime tripwires and incident logging."}],"implication":"Federal regulators should condition temporary regulatory waivers under pol-1002 on developers providing basic data logging, ensuring innovation continues without blinding downstream operators to runtime model drift."}]}"}

