iting for automated clinical breakthroughs. Market competition and domain-calibrated tort liability create real incentives for transparency far faster than top-down bureaucratic gating.\n\nPolicymakers must choose between protecting legacy market structures and liberating human capability from scarcity and disease. Mandating runtime telemetry as an ex-ante design requirement turns open innovation into an exclusive playground for trillion-dollar tech platforms. When you choose to slow deployment out of hypothetical panic, you accept the ongoing human toll of unautomated labor and untreated illness. Real safety comes from decentralized experimentation, empirical liability for actual harms, and rapid iteration in production.",
